# Clear one station's BLOCKED state, and do nothing else (ADR-0037).
#
# RESET IS NOT START. Clearing the block returns the station to STATE_WAITING —
# awaiting its own trigger — and that is the whole effect. This service must
# never plan, never send a MoveTo goal, never drive the arm home and never resume
# a belt. If the arm has to be cleared out of the way before the station can work
# again, that is a separate, deliberate operator action taken at reduced speed
# with a person present.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that is usually forgotten. The first is
# the principle: a reset that commands motion is a restart, and an unattended
# restart after an undiagnosed fault re-runs whatever caused it. The second is
# diagnostic, and it is the reason Universal Robots' own Product Alert gives —
# "automatic acknowledgement and reset of protective stops masks faults that will
# lead to a failure condition". A reset that silently re-drives the arm destroys
# the evidence of why it stopped.
#
# NONE OF THIS IS A PROTECTIVE MEASURE and it must not be described as one. It
# removes an automatic resumption. What stops an arm remains the vendor
# controller's torque limiting and physical guarding (charter §3.2).
#
# SERVICE below is the ONE place this name is written, for the same reason
# `LineState.TOPIC` is: otherwise the name is supplied to the server as a
# parameter and repeated in every client, which is a value in two places (P1) and
# is not discoverable with `ros2 interface show` (P3).

string SERVICE="/cite/line/reset_station"

# Which station to reset, by the id the L0 topology gives it. One station per
# call, never "all": STATE_BLOCKED is per-station, and a caller who has to name
# the station has to know which one they are clearing.
string station_id

---

# Whether the station was reset. False means nothing changed, at all.
bool accepted

# Why, and it is a CODE rather than a bare false — the caller must be able to
# tell "there was nothing to reset" from "this station is faulted and you may
# not", because those want opposite next actions from an operator.
#
#   SUCCESS             the station was blocked and is now WAITING
#   PRECONDITION_FAILED there was nothing to reset: the station is unknown to
#                       this line, or it is IDLE, WAITING or WORKING
#   HARDWARE_FAULT      refused. This station is FAULTED, or some other station
#                       is, and one faulted station is a faulted line. A cell in
#                       that state is not commandable, so resuming one station of
#                       it would be resuming a cell that cannot be commanded.
#                       Clearing STATE_FAULTED is deliberately out of scope: it
#                       needs a decision about what evidence makes a cell
#                       commandable again, and that belongs with the safety layer
#                       that does not exist yet.
ResultCode result

# The station's state after this call, accepted or not — a StationState.STATE_*
# value. STATE_IDLE when the station is not one this line knows about.
uint8 station_state

# The blocked reason this call cleared, echoed back because THIS IS THE ONLY
# PLACE IT SURVIVES.
#
# `LineState` cannot carry it afterwards: that message is published on the STATE
# profile — reliable but VOLATILE — and says of itself that it is "a periodic
# report of the present, not a record", so a person reading it after the fact
# gets the next message and not the last one. `StationState` has no reason field
# at all, and `LineState.blocked_reason` is a single line-level string that
# carries only the FIRST blocked station's reason — so with two stations blocked,
# one reason is already unpublished today.
#
# The operator who reset is holding this reply, which is the one place a volatile
# topic cannot lose it. The server also logs it at WARN or above, so the record
# survives in the process log whether or not anyone was subscribed.
#
# Empty when `accepted` is false.
string cleared_reason
